  "walkthroughs": [
    {
      "tag": "Plan",
      "h2": "Atkinson & Shiffrin 1968",
      "body_html": "<p><strong>Atkinson & Shiffrin (1968)</strong> proposed three stores in sequence. Information enters via <strong>sensory register</strong>; attention transfers it to <strong>STM</strong>; <strong>rehearsal</strong> transfers it to <strong>LTM</strong>. Without attention, sensory info decays; without rehearsal, STM info is lost.</p>"
    },
    {
      "tag": "Sense",
      "h2": "Sensory Register",
      "body_html": "<p><strong>Sperling (1960)</strong>: participants briefly shown 12-letter grids. Whole-report recall ~4 letters; partial report (cued by tone immediately) ~9 letters. Sensory register holds large capacity but decays in <strong>~250ms</strong> (iconic). Echoic memory lasts a few seconds longer.</p>"
    },
    {
      "tag": "Move",
      "h2": "Rehearsal & Transfer",
      "body_html": "<p>STM has limited capacity and duration. <strong>Maintenance rehearsal</strong> keeps info in STM by recycling; <strong>elaborative rehearsal</strong> links to existing LTM, deepening the trace (Craik & Lockhart 1972). Both transfer to LTM, but elaborative is more effective.</p>"
    }
  ],
